@page "/my-shifts"
@* FR-109 and FR-112: a driver going on and off duty, and the log of when they did.

   One action, not two. Whether the driver is on duty is one predicate - an open shift exists - so
   the screen offers exactly the transition that predicate allows, and a driver never meets a button
   that can only be refused. The refusals still exist and still decide: SHIFT_ALREADY_OPEN and
   SHIFT_NOT_OPEN are raised by IShiftService whatever this screen drew (FR-12).

   The rows are the caller's own, and that is the guard's answer rather than this screen's. There is
   no driver column and no driver filter: IAccessGuard.RequireShiftScope narrows the list to the
   caller's own driver row before the query is built, so a driver cannot be shown somebody else's
   shift by a mistake made here (AD-3).

   The Roles argument routes a wrong role to /access-denied (FR-79). A dispatcher is sent there on
   purpose: their scope narrows nothing, so this screen would show them the whole roster under a
   heading that says "mine" - which is the same reason MyDeliveries refuses them.

   The @if blocks below are independent rather than if/else chains: an `else` at markup level reads
   to NFR-14's source scan as a rendered English word. *@
@attribute [Authorize(Roles = "Driver")]
@inject IShiftService Duty
@inject ICurrentUser Caller
@inject IStringLocalizer<UiText> Localizer
@inject IStringLocalizer<ErrorMessages> Errors
@inherits CircuitScopedComponent

<PageTitle>@Localizer["MyShiftsTitle"]</PageTitle>

<h1>
    <Icon Name="IconName.Shifts" />@Localizer["MyShiftsTitle"]
</h1>

@* FR-110's refusals arrive here: already on duty, or not on duty at all. Resolved through the same
   catalogue the REST envelope uses, so the two cannot disagree (NFR-3). *@
@foreach (var key in _failures)
{
    <div class="alert alert-danger" role="alert">@Errors[key]</div>
}

<div class="row g-3 align-items-end mb-3">
    @* The one place on either screen where the subject really is the driver: this is a claim about
       the caller, right now, rather than a label on a row in a history - so it wears the pickers'
       wording ("на зміні") and not ShiftStateLabel's, which says whether a shift is open or over.
       Two independent @@if blocks with literal keys, for the reason every other block here is
       written that way. *@
    <div class="col-sm-auto">
        <span class="form-label d-block">@Localizer["ShiftState"]</span>
        @if (Open is not null)
        {
            <span class="badge text-bg-success">@Localizer["ShiftOpen"]</span>
        }
        @if (Open is null)
        {
            <span class="badge text-bg-secondary">@Localizer["ShiftClosed"]</span>
        }
    </div>
    @* Exactly one of the two, decided by the same predicate the badge above reads. Both are
       withheld from a driver whose claims carry no driver row id: there is no row to attach a shift
       to, and IAccessGuard refuses them anyway - the list read above has already said so in the
       banner. *@
    @if (_driverId is not null && Open is null)
    {
        <div class="col-sm-auto">
            <button type="button" class="btn btn-success dt-shift-start" @onclick="StartAsync">
                <Icon Name="IconName.Confirm" />@Localizer["GoOnDuty"]
            </button>
        </div>
    }
    @if (_driverId is not null && Open is not null)
    {
        <div class="col-sm-auto">
            <button type="button" class="btn btn-primary dt-shift-end" @onclick="EndAsync">
                <Icon Name="IconName.Close" />@Localizer["GoOffDuty"]
            </button>
        </div>
    }
</div>

@* NFR-27 keeps paging, and this screen asks for the first page - so past a full page the rest of
   the driver's history is not shown. Saying so is the difference between a limit and a screen that
   quietly lies about how long somebody has worked. *@
@if (IsTruncated)
{
    <div class="alert alert-warning" role="status">@Localizer["ListTruncated"]</div>
}

<DtDataTable TItem="ShiftSummary"
             Items="_shifts"
             IsLoading="_loading"
             EmptyText="@Localizer["ShiftsEmpty"]">
    <HeaderTemplate>
        <th scope="col">@Localizer["ShiftStartedAt"]</th>
        <th scope="col">@Localizer["ShiftEndedAt"]</th>
        <th scope="col">@Localizer["ShiftState"]</th>
        <th scope="col">@Localizer["Actions"]</th>
    </HeaderTemplate>
    <RowTemplate Context="shift">
        <td>@Moment(shift.StartedAt)</td>
        <td>
            @if (shift.EndedAt is null)
            {
                <span>@Localizer["ShiftStillOpen"]</span>
            }
            @if (shift.EndedAt is not null)
            {
                <span>@Moment(shift.EndedAt.Value)</span>
            }
        </td>
        <td>
            <ShiftStateLabel IsOpen="shift.IsOpen" />
        </td>
        <td class="dt-row-actions">
            @* The handlers are built in code rather than written as lambdas here: a `=>` inside an
               attribute closes the tag as far as a source scan is concerned. *@
            <button type="button" class="btn btn-primary btn-sm dt-shift-edit" @onclick="EditHandler(shift)">
                <Icon Name="IconName.Edit" />@Localizer["Edit"]
            </button>
            <button type="button" class="btn btn-danger btn-sm dt-shift-delete" @onclick="DeleteHandler(shift)">
                <Icon Name="IconName.Delete" />@Localizer["Delete"]
            </button>
        </td>
    </RowTemplate>
</DtDataTable>

@* FR-85: the shared dialog. FR-112 gives a driver their own corrections - a shift they started an
   hour late is theirs to fix - and the guard is what keeps it to their own. *@
<DtDialog @ref="_formDialog" Title="@Localizer["ShiftForm"]">
    <ChildContent>
        @* The same list as the one at the top of the page, and deliberately a second copy: a
           DtDialog opens with showModal(), so a refusal rendered only up there is a message behind
           an inert backdrop. *@
        @foreach (var key in _failures)
        {
            <div class="alert alert-danger" role="alert">@Errors[key]</div>
        }

        <EditForm id="my-shift-form" Model="_form" OnValidSubmit="SaveAsync">
            @* Clearing a date field leaves a parse failure on the EditContext and OnValidSubmit
               never fires, so without this the Save button would do nothing and say nothing. *@
            <ValidationSummary />

            <div class="mb-3">
                <label class="form-label" for="my-shift-started-at">@Localizer["ShiftStartedAt"]</label>
                <InputDate id="my-shift-started-at" class="form-control"
                           Type="InputDateType.DateTimeLocal"
                           ParsingErrorMessage="@Localizer["DateParseError"]"
                           @bind-Value="_form.StartedAt" />
            </div>
            <div class="mb-3">
                <label class="form-label" for="my-shift-ended-at">@Localizer["ShiftEndedAt"]</label>
                <InputDate id="my-shift-ended-at" class="form-control"
                           Type="InputDateType.DateTimeLocal"
                           ParsingErrorMessage="@Localizer["DateParseError"]"
                           @bind-Value="_form.EndedAt" />
                @* FR-117 said in a sentence: emptying this box leaves a finished shift finished. A
                   shift is ended by the action above, once, and never un-ended. *@
                <div class="form-text">@Localizer["ShiftEndedAtHint"]</div>
            </div>
        </EditForm>
    </ChildContent>
    <Actions>
        @* Disabled while a save is in flight: the circuit yields at the await, so a second click
           would send the same correction again. *@
        <button type="submit" form="my-shift-form" class="btn btn-primary dt-shift-save"
                disabled="@_saving">
            <Icon Name="IconName.Confirm" />@Localizer["Save"]
        </button>
        <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary dt-shift-cancel" @onclick="CloseFormAsync">
            <Icon Name="IconName.Close" />@Localizer["Cancel"]
        </button>
    </Actions>
</DtDialog>

@* FR-81 and NFR-22: a destructive action is confirmed through the shared prompt, a driver's own as
   much as a dispatcher's. *@
<DtConfirmDialog @ref="_deleteDialog"
                 Title="@Localizer["DeleteShiftTitle"]"
                 Message="@Localizer["DeleteShiftMessage"]"
                 OnConfirmed="ConfirmDeleteAsync" />

@code {
    private IReadOnlyList<ShiftSummary> _shifts = [];
    private IReadOnlyList<string> _failures = [];
    private ShiftForm _form = new();
    private DriverId? _driverId;
    private ShiftSummary? _pendingDeletion;
    private DtDialog? _formDialog;
    private DtConfirmDialog? _deleteDialog;
    private bool _loading = true;

    /// <summary>
    /// True while a save is in flight, which is what disables the submit button for as long as it
    /// lasts. The circuit yields at the await, so without it a second click would send the same
    /// correction again.
    /// </summary>
    private bool _saving;

    /// <summary>
    /// The caller's open shift, or null when they are off duty.
    /// <para>
    /// Read off the page the table already holds rather than asked for separately: open is
    /// <c>EndedAt IS NULL</c> and the index allows at most one such row per driver, so the answer is
    /// already on screen and a second query would only be a second thing that can disagree.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private ShiftSummary? Open => _shifts.FirstOrDefault(shift => shift.IsOpen);

    /// <summary>
    /// True when the page came back full, which is as much as this screen can know: the query
    /// returns no total (PRD section 8 excludes one).
    /// </summary>
    private bool IsTruncated => _shifts.Count >= ListShiftsQueryValidator.MaximumLimit;

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        // The circuit outlives the request that opened it, so the cookie can be gone by the time a
        // screen calls a service. Raising the contract's own code here is what puts that case
        // through FR-13's single boundary instead of a per-screen redirect.
        if (!Caller.IsAuthenticated)
        {
            throw new ForbiddenException(
                ErrorCode.AUTH_UNAUTHENTICATED,
                "The circuit has no authenticated caller: the session cookie is gone.");
        }

        // Read once, after the authenticated check: ICurrentUser throws for an anonymous caller, so
        // asking outside that branch would take the screen down instead of refusing it.
        _driverId = Caller.DriverId;

        await LoadAsync();
    }

    /// <summary>The row's edit action, bound as a delegate so no lambda has to live in markup.</summary>
    private Func<Task> EditHandler(ShiftSummary shift) => () => OpenEditAsync(shift);

    /// <summary>The row's delete action, for the same reason.</summary>
    private Func<Task> DeleteHandler(ShiftSummary shift) => () => OpenDeleteAsync(shift);

    private async Task LoadAsync()
    {
        _loading = true;

        // Rendered before the await, or the flag is set and cleared inside one synchronous stretch
        // and DtDataTable's loading row never reaches the browser on a post-write refetch.
        StateHasChanged();

        try
        {
            // No driver in the query: the narrowing is the guard's, and naming one here would be a
            // second place deciding whose rows a driver may see (AD-3).
            _shifts = await Duty.ListAsync(
                new ListShiftsQuery(0, ListShiftsQueryValidator.MaximumLimit, null),
                CircuitToken);
        }
        catch (DriveTrackException failure)
        {
            _failures = FailureKeys.For(failure);
        }
        finally
        {
            // In a finally so a refused or failed read leaves the table in its empty state rather
            // than spinning forever, which reads to a user as a hung screen.
            _loading = false;
        }
    }

    private async Task StartAsync()
    {
        if (_driverId is not { } driverId)
        {
            return;
        }

        try
        {
            // No instant in the command: going on duty is stamped by the server's clock, so a
            // driver cannot report having started an hour ago by pressing the button now (AD-13).
            await Duty.StartAsync(new StartShiftCommand(driverId), CircuitToken);

            _failures = [];
        }
        catch (DriveTrackException failure)
        {
            _failures = FailureKeys.For(failure);

            return;
        }

        // Outside the try: the shift has been opened, and a failed refresh afterwards must not be
        // reported as a driver who never went on duty.
        await LoadAsync();
    }

    private async Task EndAsync()
    {
        if (_driverId is not { } driverId)
        {
            return;
        }

        try
        {
            await Duty.EndAsync(new EndShiftCommand(driverId), CircuitToken);

            _failures = [];
        }
        catch (DriveTrackException failure)
        {
            _failures = FailureKeys.For(failure);

            return;
        }

        await LoadAsync();
    }

    private async Task OpenEditAsync(ShiftSummary shift)
    {
        _failures = [];
        _form = new ShiftForm
        {
            Id = shift.Id,

            // Local time, because the control shows local time and the circuit runs on the server -
            // the same reading every other screen gives a stored instant (NFR-15).
            StartedAt = shift.StartedAt.ToLocalTime(),
            EndedAt = shift.EndedAt?.ToLocalTime(),
        };

        if (_formDialog is not null)
        {
            await _formDialog.ShowAsync();
        }
    }

    private async Task CloseFormAsync()
    {
        if (_formDialog is not null)
        {
            await _formDialog.CloseAsync();
        }
    }

    private async Task SaveAsync()
    {
        if (_form.Id is not { } shiftId)
        {
            return;
        }

        if (_saving)
        {
            return;
        }

        // Set before the await and cleared in the finally: the button is disabled from the moment
        // the first click is handled until the server has answered, so a second click has nothing
        // to act on.
        _saving = true;

        try
        {
            await Duty.UpdateAsync(shiftId, _form.AsUpdate(), CircuitToken);

            _failures = [];

            await CloseFormAsync();
        }
        catch (DriveTrackException failure)
        {
            _failures = FailureKeys.For(failure);

            return;
        }
        finally
        {
            _saving = false;
        }

        // Outside the try, deliberately. The write has committed, so a failure refreshing the list
        // afterwards is a failed read, and reporting it as a refused save would tell the driver
        // their correction was not saved when it was.
        await LoadAsync();
    }

    private async Task OpenDeleteAsync(ShiftSummary shift)
    {
        _failures = [];
        _pendingDeletion = shift;

        if (_deleteDialog is not null)
        {
            await _deleteDialog.ShowAsync();
        }
    }

    private async Task ConfirmDeleteAsync()
    {
        if (_pendingDeletion is not { } shift)
        {
            return;
        }

        _pendingDeletion = null;

        try
        {
            await Duty.DeleteAsync(shift.Id, CircuitToken);

            _failures = [];
        }
        catch (DriveTrackException failure)
        {
            _failures = FailureKeys.For(failure);

            return;
        }

        await LoadAsync();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// An instant as this screen shows it. Local time against the current culture (NFR-15).
    /// </summary>
    private static string Moment(DateTimeOffset value) =>
        value.ToLocalTime().ToString("g", System.Globalization.CultureInfo.CurrentCulture);

    /// <summary>
    /// What the dialog is editing. A screen-local shape rather than a command: a command's update
    /// fields are <c>Optional&lt;T&gt;</c>, and two-way binding needs somewhere plain to write.
    /// </summary>
    private sealed class ShiftForm
    {
        /// <summary>The shift being corrected. Null means there is nothing to save.</summary>
        public int? Id { get; set; }

        /// <summary>When it started, in local time because that is what the control shows.</summary>
        public DateTimeOffset? StartedAt { get; set; }

        /// <summary>When it ended, or null while it is open.</summary>
        public DateTimeOffset? EndedAt { get; set; }

        /// <summary>
        /// The correction this form describes (AD-23, FR-117).
        /// <para>
        /// An empty box is <em>absent</em> rather than a present null:
        /// <c>Optional&lt;DateTimeOffset&gt;</c> has no case for a clear, so emptying the end box
        /// leaves a finished shift finished instead of reopening it.
        /// </para>
        /// </summary>
        public UpdateShiftCommand AsUpdate() =>
            new(
                StartedAt is { } start
                    ? Optional<DateTimeOffset>.Present(start)
                    : Optional<DateTimeOffset>.Absent,
                EndedAt is { } end
                    ? Optional<DateTimeOffset>.Present(end)
                    : Optional<DateTimeOffset>.Absent);
    }
}
